===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|fr|réticule}}, from {{der|en|la|reticulum}}, diminutive of {{m|la|rēte|t=net}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɹɛtɪkjuːl/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-reticule.ogg|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[reticle]]; a [[grid]] in the eyepiece of an instrument. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2017|title=The Legend of WWII’s Bombsight Rapunzel|author=Eric Grundhauser
|passage=[H]er hair had been used to create the '''reticule''' in the famous Norden bombsight—a top-secret WWII targeting device.}}
# A small women's bag made of a woven net-like material. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Landon Lady Anne Granard|page=183|volume=II|passage=When reading the note, and arriving at an assurance of Helen's absence, Lady Anne had indignantly crushed it in her hand, and thrust it into her '''reticule''', but, on her return home, whilst Fanchette was industriously employed upon her hair with the invaluable liquid dye, she drew out the rumpled paper, and read the concluding paragraph.}}
#* {{RQ:Thackeray Pendennis|chapter=7|text=Major Arthur Pendennis, arrived in due time at Fairoaks, after a dreary night passed in the mail-coach, … where a widow lady, opposite, had not only shut out the fresh air by closing all the windows of the vehicle, but had filled the interior with fumes of Jamaica rum and water, which she sucked perpetually from a bottle in her''' reticule''' {{…}}}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Bleak House|chapter=1|text=She carries some small litter in a '''reticule''' which she calls her documents, principally consisting of paper matches and dry lavender.}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Tale of Two Cities|chapter=Book 3, Chapter 8|text=Miss Pross, exploring the depths of her '''reticule''' through her tears with great difficulty, paid for her wine.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1993|publisher=Penguin|year_published=1994|author=w:TC Boyle|title=The Road to Wellville|page=150|passage=Eleanor wore a green silk dress to bring out here eyes, with an ivory tatted collar and '''reticule''' to match.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=Thomas Pynchon|title=Against the Day|pageurl=https://books.google.ca/books?id=iBA0e-8kZ4cC&printsec=frontcover#v=onepage&q&f=false|page=606|publisher=Vintage|year_published=2007
|passage=Pléiade {{...}} lingered through another bottle of wine before producing from her '''reticule''' a Vacheron & Constantin watch {{...}}.}}

